    <item>
      <title>Why we moved our job queue into PostgreSQL</title>
      <link>https://blog.example.com/2026/09/postgres-job-queue/?utm_source=rss&amp;utm_medium=rss</link>
      <comments>https://blog.example.com/2026/09/postgres-job-queue/#comments</comments>
      <dc:creator><![CDATA[Jana Nováková]]></dc:creator>
      <pubDate>Fri, 25 Sep 2026 14:05:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <category><![CDATA[Databases]]></category>
      <category><![CDATA[PostgreSQL]]></category>
      <category><![CDATA[databases]]></category>
      <category><![CDATA[News &amp; Opinion]]></category>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">https://blog.example.com/?p=4812</guid>
      <description><![CDATA[<p>We replaced a separate broker with a transactional outbox&#8230;</p>]]></description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>For three years our workers pulled jobs from a separate message broker. Keeping it consistent with the database cost us more than it saved.</p>
<h2>The outbox pattern</h2>
<p>Every state change and its follow-up work are written in <strong>one transaction</strong>. Read the <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-select.html#SQL-FOR-UPDATE-SHARE">locking documentation</a> for the details.</p>
<figure><img src="https://blog.example.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/queue-diagram.png" alt="Queue diagram" width="1200" height="630" /><figcaption>Jobs flow from the outbox to the workers.</figcaption></figure>
<pre><code>SELECT * FROM outbox ORDER BY id FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED LIMIT 10;</code></pre>
<p>The post <a href="https://blog.example.com/2026/09/postgres-job-queue/">Why we moved our job queue into PostgreSQL</a> appeared first on <a href="https://blog.example.com">Example Engineering Blog</a>.</p>]]></content:encoded>
    </item>
